## 9. Insecure Deserialization

### Vulnerability Description
Deserializing untrusted data can lead to arbitrary code execution or object injection attacks.

### Examples

**Vulnerable Code:**
```javascript
// Using eval - NEVER DO THIS
const userData = req.body.data;
eval(userData); // Arbitrary code execution!

// Using pickle-like serialization
const pickle = require('node-pickle');
const obj = pickle.loads(req.body.serialized); // Unsafe!

// Using function constructor
const userFunction = new Function(req.body.code);
userFunction(); // Code execution!
```

### Mitigation Strategies

**1. JSON-Based Serialization Only:**
```javascript
// Safe approach - JSON only
app.post('/api/data', (req, res) => {
  try {
    const data = JSON.parse(req.body); // Safe parsing
    // Validate and process data
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid JSON' });
  }
});
```

**2. Schema Validation:**
```javascript
const Joi = require('joi');

const schema = Joi.object({
  name: Joi.string().alphanum().min(3).max(30).required(),
  email: Joi.string().email().required(),
  age: Joi.number().integer().min(18).max(100)
});

app.post('/user', (req, res) => {
  const { error, value } = schema.validate(req.body);
  if (error) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: error.details });
  }
  // Safe data in 'value'
});
```

**3. Avoid Dangerous Functions:**
```javascript
// Bad - Never use these with user input
// eval()
// Function()
// JSON.parse() with custom revivers that execute code
// require() with user-controlled paths
// child_process.exec() with user input

// Good alternatives
const vm = require('vm');

// If you must execute user code, use a sandboxed environment
const sandbox = { console };
vm.runInNewContext(userCode, sandbox, { timeout: 5000 });
```

